  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nSnow Showers Tonight into Monday: Flow turns to the northwest \ntonight, with back to back shortwaves moving across the northern \nGreat Lakes tonight into Monday. This will kick off a round of \nlake effect snow activity as 850mb temps drop to between -10 and -16C\nand plenty of moisture left over from the departing low pressure.\nThe most persistent activity will be in the snowbelts of north-\ncentral WI, with locations in northern Vilas Co. seeing 1-4\" of \nsnow where any dominate snow band remains in place for a few \nhours. Totals look to drop off pretty quickly as you head south, \nbut at least a dusting could spread south into parts of central WI\nand east-central WI. Chance of 1\"+ is now over 70% near the MI \nborder and over 60% for 2\", supporting the higher totals. Held off\non an advisory as only a small part of Vilas Co. looks to get \nadvisory snow, plus the ground is still on the warm side and snow \nratios should stay under 15:1, so some melting/compaction is \nexpected. Drier air filters into the region Monday afternoon/evening\nas high pressure arrives, allowing the lake effect snow activity \nto come to an end by mid-late Monday evening.\n\nThere will be a chance for some heavier snow showers or possibly a\nfew snow squalls as low-level lapse rates will steepen to 7.0-7.5\nC/km Monday morning, up to 100 J/kg of SBCAPE, the potent \nshortwave aloft, along with a brief period of stronger lift in the\nDGZ and FGEN moves across the area. The snow squall parameter is \nover one as well, so will need to monitor this potential.\n\nRest of the Week: Dry conditions prevail Tuesday and Wednesday\nas high pressure slides across Great Lakes. Amplified troughing\nwill arrive by Thanksgiving, with a closed low moving south of WI.\nQuestion is just how quickly the low turn north and if we will get\nany precip into WI. Current forecast has the system just grazing\neastern WI with some light rain/snow (10-40% chances), but will \nneed to give the models a little more time to come into some kind \nof agreement.\n\nTemperatures: Late November temps have finally arrived\n(central/northern) or will arrive tonight (elsewhere) as strong\nCAA occurs over the region tonight into Monday. Temps will fall \nto a little below normal Monday and Tuesday, with highs mainly in \nthe 30s. Then temps slowly moderate mid-late next week, with \nslightly above normal readings by Thursday or Friday.",
